---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="TDEE Calculator – Total Daily Energy Expenditure | Nawtch"
  description="Free TDEE calculator using the Mifflin-St Jeor equation. Get your total daily energy expenditure and a recommended calorie range for your goal."
  canonicalPath="/calculators/tdee/"
  bodyClass="calculators"
>
  <header>
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>TDEE Calculator</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Estimate your Total Daily Energy Expenditure and a recommended calorie range for your goal.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="ghost-btn" href="/calculators/">← Back to Calculators</a>
  </header>

  <p class="field-hint" style="margin:-4px 0 14px;">Just want a quick number without the TDEE terminology? Try our simpler <a href="/calculators/calorie/">Calorie Calculator</a>.</p>

  <div id="calc-tdee"></div>

  <div id="calc-tdee-explain" class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How We Calculate This</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        This calorie calculator estimates your BMR (calories burned at rest) using the <strong>Mifflin-St Jeor equation</strong>, widely regarded as the most accurate predictive formula for resting energy expenditure in healthy
        adults. Multiplying it by your activity level gives your <strong>TDEE</strong> — the calories needed
        to maintain your current weight. Your goal (lose, stay the same, gain) then shifts that number up or
        down to build a recommended daily range.
      </p>
      <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2305711/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the original study →</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="calc-tdee-activity" class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Choosing Your Activity Level</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        The activity multiplier moves your result more than any other input here — one level up or down can
        shift your TDEE by 400–600 calories/day — so it's worth picking the one that actually matches your week,
        not the one that sounds the most flattering. Here's what each multiplier used above means in concrete
        terms:
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Sedentary (×1.2)</strong> — little or no structured exercise, a desk job, and daily movement
        limited to normal errands. Working out once a week or less still falls here, not the level above it.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Light (×1.375)</strong> — structured exercise 1–3 days a week (gym sessions, runs, sports),
        layered on top of a mostly sedentary day job.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Moderate (×1.55)</strong> — structured exercise 3–5 days a week at real training intensity, not
        just casual walking. This is the most commonly over-selected level: "I try to work out most weeks"
        undershoots what 3–5 actual training sessions looks like.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Heavy Exercise (×1.725)</strong> — structured exercise 6–7 days a week, or a physically
        demanding job stacked on top of regular training.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Athlete (×1.9)</strong> — hard daily training combined with a physically demanding job, or
        two-a-day training sessions, the pattern typical of in-season competitive athletes. Very few people
        outside of that situation belong here.
        <br /><br />
        When you're between two levels, pick the lower one. Overestimating activity is the most common reason a
        TDEE-based target doesn't match how someone's weight actually trends — it's easy to add calories back
        once you see the data, harder to explain away a stall.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script src="../../../islands/vanilla/calculators/tdee/tdee.js"></script>
</BaseLayout>
